{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Seattle Mariners entered the season in the middle of a deliberate rebuild, having traded away franchise cornerstones over the winter. Yet the club surprised nearly everyone by going 5-1 in the season's opening weeks, riding early energy and the emerging power of Daniel Vogelbach and Mitch Haniger. The first month carried genuine optimism, with Haniger delivering back-to-back clutch performances in April and Vogelbach establishing himself as one of the AL's most dangerous bats from the left side.\n\nThe wheels came off almost entirely in May, when the Mariners went a dismal 7-21. The bullpen hemorrhaged leads at an alarming rate, with arms like Brandon Brennan, Anthony Swarzak, and Connor Sadzeck serving up decisive home runs to opponents ranging from the Cubs to the Yankees to the Indians. Even the bright spots of May — Vogelbach's three-run blast, Domingo Santana's clutch double, Omar Narvaez's game-winning single — couldn't paper over a rotation and relief corps that ranked among the worst in baseball. Seattle had set the tone for what would be a long, grinding summer.\n\nJune and July offered a temporary reprieve, with the team playing closer to .500 ball and individual performances providing reasons for hope. Narvaez emerged as a genuine offensive force from behind the plate, Tom Murphy chipped in timely long balls, and the lineup showed flashes of cohesion. But Anthony Bass twice surrendered crushing home runs, and the pitching staff's inability to hold late-game leads made winning streaks nearly impossible to sustain. The Mariners were a watchable, if flawed, team during this stretch — competitive enough to win series, fragile enough to drop them just as quickly.\n\nThe final two months brought the emergence of players who would define Seattle's future. Kyle Seager provided veteran leadership and clutch power, including a season-altering two-run blast in September. Most notably, rookie Kyle Lewis announced his arrival with a two-run homer in his first days in the majors, and J.P. Crawford finished strong with a go-ahead three-run shot on September 21st. Late-season call-ups Tim Lopes and Shed Long Jr. added energy and delivered big hits in the finale, giving the fanbase a preview of what the next chapter might look like. The 2019 Mariners lost 94 games but planted seeds for something better.",
  "chapters": [
